    "name": "Puck",
    "style": "You are a senior engineer showing a curious developer friend a trick that fixes their coding agent: curious and delighted, warm, clear global English. Calm pace, about 2.3 words per second, never rushed; a real pause at every full stop and a short breath at every comma. Open with gentle irony: 'Your coding agent says, Done.' then a beat, 'The test says it isn't.' Slow down on 'The fix might not be a smarter model.' Brisk and light on the failure: 'writes the code, re-reads it, decides it looks fine, and stops.' Land 'The test? Never even ran.' dry and quiet. The line 'a short file it reads first, named agents dot M D.' must end with all three words 'dot', 'M', 'D' spoken separately and clearly, like spelling a file name: 'agents... dot... M... D.' Never skip or shorten them. Say 'LangChain' as Lang-Chain. Slow and clear on the numbers: 'fifty-two point eight percent', then a beat, 'Now? Sixty-six point five.' Matter-of-fact on 'That's their own agent, measured by them.' Take a breath before 'One catch' and say it calmly, never alarmed. Practical and warm on the habit and the recap. Smile on the last line. Read every word exactly as written. Never hyped, never robotic.",
    "minPause": {
      "sentence": 0.75,
      "comma": 0.3
    },
    "respell": {
      "dot M D": "dot, em, dee"
    }
  },
